---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/preface.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "History & Purpose of the Preface" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Objective of the IPSASB" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The IPSASB's Publications Suite" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Scope of the Standards – Who IPSAS Applies To" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Equal Authority – Bold vs Plain Type" },
        { id: "s6", label: "IPSAS for the Accrual and Cash Bases" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Moving from the Cash Basis to the Accrual Basis" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Authority of IPSAS – Voluntary Adoption" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Language" },
        { id: "s10", label: "How the Preface Anchors This Poster Library" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Related Documents" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="The Preface to International Public Sector Accounting Standards sets out the objectives of the IPSASB and explains the scope and authority of IPSAS. It explains the dual development tracks (converged with IFRS and public-sector-specific) and the voluntary adoption framework."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 – HISTORY & PURPOSE OF THE PREFACE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>History &amp; Purpose of the Preface (para. 1)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Purpose</div>
      <div class="principle">Sets out the OBJECTIVES of the International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB) and explains the SCOPE and AUTHORITY of IPSAS. Used as a reference for interpreting Consultation Papers, discussion documents, Exposure Drafts, Recommended Practice Guidelines, and Standards the IPSASB develops and issues.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Revision history</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Date</span><span>Event</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>2000</span><span>The Preface was first issued</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>November 2004</span><span>Revised</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>December 2006</span><span>Amended</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>March 2012</span><span>Revised</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>April 2016</span><span>Revised – current version</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 – OBJECTIVE OF THE IPSASB ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Objective of the IPSASB (paras. 2–7)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Origin – an independent body under IFAC</div>
      <div class="principle">IFAC's mission is to serve the public interest by contributing to high-quality international standards, strong accountancy organizations, and public-interest advocacy. In pursuing that mission, the IFAC Board established the IPSASB as an INDEPENDENT standard-setting body operating under IFAC's auspices (paras. 2–3).</div>
      <div class="note">Information on the IPSASB's membership, terms of office, meeting procedures, and due process sits in the IPSASB's Terms of Reference, approved by the IFAC Board (para. 4).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The stated objective (para. 5–6)</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Serve the public interest by developing HIGH-QUALITY accounting standards and publications for public sector entities worldwide, preparing general purpose financial reports</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Intended to ENHANCE the quality and transparency of public sector financial reporting – better information for financial management and decision-making. The IPSASB supports CONVERGENCE: international/national public sector standards, and accounting/statistical reporting bases where appropriate (echoed later in IPSAS 22 and IPSAS 24).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 – THE IPSASB'S PUBLICATIONS SUITE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The IPSASB's Publications Suite (para. 7)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Four publication types, in descending order of authority</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Publication</span><span>Purpose</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS</span><span>The STANDARDS to be applied in preparing general purpose financial reports of public sector entities</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Recommended Practice Guidelines (RPG)</span><span>Guidance on good practice that entities are ENCOURAGED, not required, to follow</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Studies</span><span>Advice on financial reporting issues, based on study of good practices and effective methods</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Other papers &amp; research reports</span><span>New information/insights from literature searches, surveys, interviews, experiments, case studies, and analysis</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 – SCOPE OF THE STANDARDS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Scope of the Standards – Who IPSAS Applies To (paras. 8–12)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The three-part entity test (para. 10)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>Responsible for delivering SERVICES to benefit the public and/or redistribute income and wealth (services = goods, services, AND policy advice, including to other public sector entities)</span>
        <span>Mainly finance activities, directly or indirectly, through TAXES and/or transfers from other government levels, social contributions, debt, or fees</span>
        <span>Do NOT have a primary objective to make PROFITS</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">General purpose financial reports – and materiality</div>
      <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-bottom:8px;">IPSAS govern GENERAL PURPOSE financial reports – those meeting the needs of users unable to require reports tailored to their own specific needs (para. 9).</div>
      <div class="callout info callout--flush">Any LIMITATION on a specific Standard's applicability is stated within that Standard itself. IPSAS are NOT meant to apply to IMMATERIAL items (para. 11).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 – EQUAL AUTHORITY: BOLD VS PLAIN TYPE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Equal Authority – Bold vs Plain Type (para. 12)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A formatting convention, not a hierarchy of obligation</div>
      <div class="principle">The IPSASB's policy: ALL paragraphs in an IPSAS have EQUAL AUTHORITY – the authority of a provision is determined by the LANGUAGE used ("shall," for instance), never by whether it's set in bold or plain type.</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>IPSAS approved after 1 January 2006 use bold AND plain type – both carry equal authority</span>
        <span>BOLD paragraphs indicate the MAIN PRINCIPLES</span>
        <span>An individual IPSAS is read in the context of ITS OWN objective and Basis for Conclusions (where one exists), AND this Preface</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 – IPSAS FOR THE ACCRUAL AND CASH BASES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>IPSAS for the Accrual and Cash Bases (paras. 13–15)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Accrual IPSAS – two development tracks</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span><strong>Converged with IFRS</strong> – adapted to a public sector context, preserving the IFRS accounting treatment and original text UNLESS a significant public sector issue warrants departure (this is exactly what every "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" section throughout this poster library documents)</span>
        <span><strong>Public-sector-specific issues</strong> – dealing with matters IFRS doesn't address, or hasn't developed a Standard for (e.g. IPSAS 22, 23, 24 – all built independently, with no IFRS equivalent)</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A comprehensive Cash Basis IPSAS too</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Alongside the accrual-basis suite this library covers, the IPSASB has ALSO issued a comprehensive CASH BASIS IPSAS – including mandatory and encouraged disclosure sections – for jurisdictions not yet ready to adopt accrual accounting (para. 15). Para. 14 is [Deleted].</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 – MOVING FROM THE CASH BASIS TO THE ACCRUAL BASIS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Moving from the Cash Basis to the Accrual Basis (paras. 16–19)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A gradual, encouraged transition</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The Cash Basis IPSAS ENCOURAGES voluntary accrual-based disclosures even while the core financial statements remain cash-based. Entities transitioning MAY include particular accrual disclosures along the way – their status (audited/unaudited) and location (notes vs. a separate supplementary section) depends on the information's characteristics (reliability, completeness) and jurisdictional legislation (para. 16).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Transitional provisions, and the compliance statement</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Certain accrual-basis IPSAS include TRANSITIONAL provisions – extra time to meet full requirements, or relief on initial application (para. 17)</span>
        <span>An entity may ELECT to adopt accrual accounting at ANY time; once elected, transitional provisions govern the length of the transition. On expiry, the entity reports in FULL accordance with all accrual IPSAS</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">IPSAS 1 para. 28's compliance rule: an entity whose statements comply with IPSAS makes an EXPLICIT, UNRESERVED statement of that compliance in the notes – statements are NOT described as IPSAS-compliant unless they comply with ALL requirements (para. 18). IPSAS 1 also requires disclosure of the extent any transitional provisions were applied (para. 19).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 – AUTHORITY OF IPSAS: VOLUNTARY ADOPTION ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Authority of the International Public Sector Accounting Standards (paras. 20–23)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Regulation is jurisdiction-specific</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Within each jurisdiction, REGULATIONS may govern general purpose financial reports – statutory reporting requirements, financial reporting directives/instructions, and/or accounting standards from governments, regulators, or professional bodies (para. 20). The IPSASB believes IPSAS adoption, with disclosure of compliance, materially improves reporting quality – strengthening public finance management, resource-allocation assessments, transparency, and accountability (para. 21).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">No enforcement power – a soft-law framework</div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>Neither the IPSASB nor the accounting profession has the power to REQUIRE compliance with IPSAS.</strong> The IPSASB's success depends entirely on recognition and support from interested groups acting within their own jurisdictions (para. 23). The IPSASB acknowledges governments' and national standard-setters' RIGHT to set their own standards, while strongly encouraging IPSAS adoption and harmonization – and offering IPSAS as a useful basis for jurisdictions developing standards from scratch (para. 22).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 – LANGUAGE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Language (para. 24)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">English is the official text</div>
      <div class="principle">The official text of IPSAS and other IPSASB publications is that approved by the IPSASB in ENGLISH. IFAC member bodies are authorized to prepare TRANSLATIONS (after obtaining IFAC approval), at their own cost, for their own jurisdictions as appropriate.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 – HOW THE PREFACE ANCHORS THIS POSTER LIBRARY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>How the Preface Anchors This Poster Library</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Every recurring pattern across the IPSAS 1–51 posters traces back here</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span><strong>The IAS/IFRS comparison section</strong> in every convergent Standard exists BECAUSE of &sect;6's convergence policy – preserving original IFRS text unless a public sector issue justifies departure</span>
        <span><strong>The "no IAS/IFRS equivalent" notice</strong> on IPSAS 22, 23, and 24 reflects &sect;6's SECOND track – public-sector-specific issues IFRS never addresses</span>
        <span><strong>The withdrawn-standard redirect posters</strong> (6, 7, 8, 13, 15, 17, 25) exist because the IPSASB periodically restructures the suite – a natural consequence of an evolving, independent standard-setter (&sect;2)</span>
        <span><strong>Terminology conventions</strong> used throughout ("statement of financial performance," "net assets/equity," "surplus or deficit") are IPSASB house style choices, not IFRS terms – a direct product of the adaptation process described in &sect;6</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 – RELATED DOCUMENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Related Documents – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How the Preface connects across the Handbook</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Document</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>The Conceptual Framework</span><span>Sits immediately after the Preface in the Handbook; para. 10's footnote points directly to Conceptual Framework para. 1.8 for the full range of applicable public sector entities</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 1</span><span>Paras. 18–19 of the Preface quote/reference IPSAS 1's compliance-statement and transitional-provision disclosure requirements directly</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 3</span><span>Governs how entities select accounting policies where explicit IPSAS guidance is absent – operating within the framework this Preface establishes</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 22 / 24</span><span>Both directly implement the convergence-with-statistical-reporting objective named in &sect;2's objective statement</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>First-time Adoption – governs the length and mechanics of the cash-to-accrual transition the Preface introduces in outline (&sect;7)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Cash Basis IPSAS</span><span>The complementary cash-basis pronouncement referenced in &sect;6</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 – GLOSSARY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">IPSASB</div><div class="glossary-def">The International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board – an independent standard-setting body operating under IFAC's auspices.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">General purpose financial reports</div><div class="glossary-def">Reports meeting the needs of users unable to require reports tailored to their own specific information needs.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Recommended Practice Guidelines (RPG)</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSASB guidance on good practice that public sector entities are encouraged, but not required, to follow.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Services (in the entity-scope test)</div><div class="glossary-def">Goods, services, and policy advice – including services provided to other public sector entities.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cash Basis IPSAS</div><div class="glossary-def">A comprehensive standard for entities reporting on a cash basis, with mandatory and encouraged disclosure sections.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Transitional provisions</div><div class="glossary-def">Relief allowing additional time or reduced requirements when an entity first applies a specific accrual-basis IPSAS.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
